insert into matcha_articles (url, lang, prefecture_code, title, body, images, published_at, place)
values
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/2563', E'en', 1, E'Niseko Guide 2026: Best Ski Resorts, Things to Do, Dining', E'Hokkaido\'s Niseko area is bustling with visitors to Japan especially during the winter. There are many places worth visiting including the four ski resorts at the base of Niseko Annupuri. This article will help you to explore every corner of Niseko.\n\nVisit our Hokkaido Travel Guide for curated itineraries, seasonal activities, must-try food, and top-rated hotels.\n\nNiseko generally refers to the mountainous area in the southern part of the Shakotan Peninsula in southwest Hokkaido. It\'s also a famous ski resort that attracts skiers and snowboarders from around the world during the winter months.\n\nThe goal of visitors is the powder snow that falls on Niseko Annupuri—a 1,308 meter peak at the eastern end of the Niseko mountain range. Seasonal winds blowing from the Eurasian Continent pick up moisture from the Japan Sea, creating high-quality snow.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/12/13-156805.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/old_thumbnails/200x2000/1636.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/04-78683.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/04-78678.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2016-11-01'::timestamptz, E'Niseko'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/2597', E'en', 1, E'Hakodate: 20 Things to Do in Hokkaido\'s Famous Port City', E'Discover 20 top-rated places to visit in Hakodate, Hokkaido - from Mt. Hakodate ropeway and Goryokaku Tower to the best spots for seafood and souvenir shopping.\n\nHakodate sits near the southern tip of Hokkaido, long celebrated as a thriving international trade port. This rich history is still visible today in the city’s unique architecture, which beautifully blends European and global influences with traditional Japanese design.\n\nBeyond its historic charm, Hakodate is famous for its breathtaking night views—often cited as some of the best in the world—and its exceptionally fresh seafood.\n\nIn this guide, we’ll explore 20 must-visit spots and top things to do, along with essential travel tips on weather and how to get there.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2018/06/01-55479.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/10/12-148451.jpeg","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/10/12-148452.jpeg","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2022/01/28-121780.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2016-10-23'::timestamptz, E'Hakodate'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/6639', E'en', 1, E'Kanemori Red Brick Warehouse in Hakodate: Photo Spots and Souvenirs', E'Explore Hakodate\'s Kanemori Red Brick Warehouse! Discover the best photo spots, souvenirs, and historic architecture at this must-visit Hokkaido harbor spot.\n\nKanemori Red Brick Warehouse, located by Hakodate Harbor, within walking distance from JR Hakodate Station and the Hakodate Morning Market, is a must-visit place in Hokkaido\'s most famous port city.\n\nIts historic architecture offers a unique charm that shifts from day to night. From must-see photo spots to exclusive souvenirs, this guide covers everything you need for the perfect visit.\n\nFounded in 1887 by Kumashiro Watanabe—a visionary businessman—the Kanemori Red Brick Warehouse was later rebuilt in 1909 following a devastating fire.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2018/09/28-63660.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2018/09/28-63663.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2018/09/28-63664.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2018/09/28-63661.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2026-03-16'::timestamptz, E'Hakodate'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/3970', E'en', 2, E'Aomori Nebuta Festival 2026: Dates and Highlights', E'The Aomori Nebuta Festival is a vibrant celebration held annually in northeastern Japan, featuring giant glowing floats. Explore the 2026 dates and highlights.\n\nIt is said that the Nebuta Festival was originally based on the toro nagashi, a tradition of releasing lanterns (toro) down the river and into the sea on the night of the Tanabata Star Festival(*1), with participants praying for good health. In the Tohoku region, this tradition was referred to as "neburi nagashi," which later became condensed to nebuta, the festival name that is currently used today.\n\nThen, in the middle of the Edo period, about 1716, people started dancing in the festival with lanterns in their hands and decorated floats called "dashi" were also introduced. It wasn’t until the late Edo period that gigantic lantern floats, inspired by the art of kabuki theater, first appeared and livened up the summertime festival.\n\nAnother unique trademark of the Nebuta Festival is the boisterous dance performed by dancers called haneto. This article introduces information regarding the annual dates and venues of the Aomori Nebuta Festival, along with some tips on how to enjoy it to the fullest.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/04/03-23160.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/04/03-23157.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/04/03-23158.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/04/03-23159.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2019-04-10'::timestamptz, E'Aomori'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/23228', E'en', 2, E'Aomori Food Guide: Tsugaru Ramen, Grilled Shellfish with Miso, and More', E'This article introduces signature dishes and popular restaurants in Aomori. Surrounded by the sea, Aomori offers a wide variety of local delicacies, including Tsugaru ramen, grilled shellfish with miso, miso curry milk ramen, and more!\n\nAomori is known for its excellent local dishes such as Tsugaru Ramen, Miso Curry Milk Ramen, Oma Tuna, Grilled Shellfish with Miso, and Towada Barayaki.\n\nSurrounded by the Sea of Japan, Tsugaru Strait, and the Pacific Ocean on three sides, Aomori Prefecture features many local delicacies that utilize seafood.\n\nThis article introduces the characteristics of Aomori’s representative local dishes and popular restaurants where you can taste them.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/12/13-215205.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/12/13-215196.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/12/13-215197.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/12/13-215206.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2025-01-15'::timestamptz, E'Tsugaru'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/4255', E'en', 2, E'Cherry Blossoms in Late April! Top 15 Spots in Northern Japan', E'Discover the 15 best cherry blossom spots in Tohoku, northern Japan, for 2026, with our updated bloom forecast and travel guide for Aomori, Morioka, Kakunodate, and other iconic locations.\n\nCherry blossom forecast map created based on information released by JMC\n\nThe Tohoku region refers to the six prefectures in the northern part of Japan\'s Honshu Island: Aomori, Iwate, Miyagi, Akita, Yamagata, and Fukushima.\n\nDue to its location at a higher latitude, the cherry blossoms in Tohoku bloom a little later than in Tokyo, usually from early April to late May each year.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/03/02-260098.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2020/01/07-94518.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2020/01/07-94519.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/03/28-175574.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2024-04-16'::timestamptz, E'Aomori'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/7375', E'en', 3, E'Iwate Travel Guide: 18 Places to Visit, Food, Scenic Sites', E'Iwate is best known for the World Heritage Sites of Chusonji Temple and Motsuji Temple. It also boasts stunning nature such as the Geibikei and Genbikei gorges. Enjoy them with its delicious local cuisine, such as Wanko Soba and fresh seafood from the Pacific Ocean.\n\nIwate, located in the Tohoku region in northern Japan, is the country\'s second largest prefecture and famous for its breathtaking natural scenery.\n\nMorioka, one of the largest cities in Iwate, can be reached by Shinkansen from Tokyo in 2 hours and 13 minutes.\n\nIwate boasts World Heritage sites that date back to the era when the Fujiwara clan ruled the Tohoku region during the 12th century. The famous Japanese folklorist, Kunio Yanagita, wrote folk tales while in Iwate.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/10-76564.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/10-76566.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/10-76565.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/10-76568.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2019-06-03'::timestamptz, E'Iwate'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/7874', E'en', 3, E'Iwate Food Guide - Delicious Seafood, Noodles, Wagyu Beef, And Sweets', E'Iwate, one of the venues of the Rugby World Cup Japan 2019™️, has no shortage of tasty cuisine. We\'ve compiled a list of classic favorites such as local noodle dishes, seafood, and wagyu beef, all the way to craft beers and delicious sweets.\n\nIwate, a prefecture in the Tohoku Region, is brimming with a variety of delicious dishes!\n\nThough Iwate is famous for unique fare such as The Three Great Noodle Dishes of Morioka, visitors can enjoy the bounty of both the sea and the mountains as the prefecture\'s east side faces the Pacific Ocean and is also home to Mt. Iwate and Mt. Hachimantai. Of course, you can\'t overlook the delicious craft beers and sweets here, too.\n\nIwate is also one of the venues hosting the Rugby World Cup Japan 2019™️ (from here on referred to as the World Cup).', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/09/18-86335.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/09/08-85516.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/09/08-85517.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/09/08-85519.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2019-09-28'::timestamptz, E'Iwate'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/7824', E'en', 3, E'7 Unforgettably Scenic Spots In Iwate, Northern Japan', E'Iwate Prefecture in northern Japan is a treasure trove of scenic outdoor destinations. This article suggests seven nature-filled places, including its picturesque coastlines and turquoise-blue underground lakes. The article also features Kamaishi, a host city for the Rugby World Cup 2019.\n\nIwate Prefecture in Tohoku Region\'s is Japan\'s second largest prefecture in terms of land area and offers visitors gorgeous views of the ocean and mountains. In other words, it\'s truly a treasure trove of scenic spots.\n\nThis article introduces seven scenic destinations in Iwate Prefecture by the coastline and inland areas. We also feature sightseeing locations near Kamaishi City, one of the host cities for Rugby World Cup Japan 2019™️ (from here on referred to as the World Cup).\n\nWe start off by first introducing scenic spots located along Iwate Prefecture\'s coastline, which includes the city of Kamaishi, one of the venues for the World Cup in 2019.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/08/23-84426.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/08/23-84427.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/08/23-84431.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/08/23-84430.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2019-09-09'::timestamptz, E'Iwate'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/3535', E'en', 4, E'Sendai Travel Guide: 20 Places to Visit, Events, Food', E'Explore the best things to do in Sendai--from the historic ruins of Sendai Castle and the Zuihoden Mausoleum to nature and festivals. Discover local food, top sightseeing spots, and easy transportation tips from Tokyo.\n\nSendai, in Miyagi Prefecture, is the most populated city in the Tohoku region. It is the heart of the government, economy, and culture of this region.\n\nIn the outskirts of the city, you can go to the hot spring districts of Akiu Onsen and Sakunami Onsen, or travel 30 minutes by train to Matsushima, an area boasting one of Japan’s three most scenic views. The city is also convenient when sightseeing in the Tohoku region.\n\nThe city’s history begins from the construction of Sendai Castle in 1600 by Date Masamune (1567-1636), a daimyo (feudal lord) during the Sengoku period. He was a popular military commander and many people came to visit the area associated with him.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/02/15-71440.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/03/20-73467.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2016/11/23-10025.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/03/25-73736.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2017-01-03'::timestamptz, E'Matsushima'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/7757', E'en', 4, E'19 Spots to Visit in Miyagi – Sendai, Naruko, Ishinomaki, and More!', E'Miyagi prefecture located in the northeastern part of Japan, is known for its beautiful coastline, picturesque landscapes, and cultural attractions. Additionally, you can enjoy the city of Sendai, which is the capital of Miyagi Prefecture and a vibrant hub of the region.\n\nMiyagi Prefecture is located in the Tohoku region. It looks out onto the Pacific Ocean to the east. Mountains, like the Zao Mountain Range, and nature parks are located to the west. Miyagi is a great place to enjoy the great outdoors. In the summertime, hike up the mountains covered in fresh greenery. During the winter, you can go skiing and snowboarding.\n\nThis same prefecture suffered damage from the tsunami during the Great East Japan Earthquake in March 2011. Reconstruction is still ongoing, with cities actively involved in revitalization of the region. We introduce the highlights of Miyagi divided by its five areas: Sendai, Matsushima and Ishinomaki, Zao, Naruko, and, Akiu and Sakunami Onsen.\n\nSendai is located in the center of Miyagi and is the prefecture\'s main transportation hub. Japanese history buffs may know about Date Masamune (1567-1636), a Sengoku-era military commander with ties to Sendai. There are numerous historic sites in the city that are related to Masamune, such as Zuihoden.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/10/23-88826.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/10/23-88825.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/09/26-86756.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/10/21-88753.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2019-11-09'::timestamptz, E'Matsushima'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/23552', E'en', 4, E'Sendai Cherry Blossom Guide: 8 Scenic Spots in 2026', E'Sendai is home to many famous cherry blossom spots, including Nishi Park and the Sendai Castle Ruins. Discover eight iconic locations.\n\nCherry blossom forecast map created based on information released by JMC\n\nKnown as the "City of Trees," Sendai is surrounded by lush greenery. The city is also home to numerous famous cherry blossom spots.\n\nIn 2026, the cherry blossoms in Sendai are expected to start blooming around April 4 and reach full bloom around April 9. (Source: JMC Sakura Forecast)', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/03/02-260098.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/01/17-219608.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/01/14-219106.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/01/14-219107.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2025-01-22'::timestamptz, E'Sendai'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/3183', E'en', 5, E'Northern Japan in Autumn: 15 Fall Foliage Spots in 2025', E'In northern Japan, the fall foliage can be enjoyed earlier than Tokyo and Kyoto - from around mid-October. Learn the best 15 fall spots in Sendai, Aomori, Lake Towada, Fukushima, and other areas in Tohoku.\n\n2025 fall foliage map based on information from Weathernews\n\nThe Tohoku region, which encompasses six prefectures in the northeastern part of Japan\'s Honshu Island, is home to both magnificent natural scenery and historical structures.\n\nShirakami-Sanchi—a World Heritage Site in Aomori, Lake Towada, and the countless cultural heritage sites in Iwate\'s Hiraizumi are very beautiful in the fall.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/10/07-246219.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/07/29-142187.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/08/01-82825.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/08/01-82751.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2019-04-10'::timestamptz, null),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/4198', E'en', 5, E'Akita Kanto Festival 2026: a Parade of 10,000 Lanterns', E'The Akita Kanto Festival is held August 3-6 to pray for health and a good harvest. Check the dates and highlights to witness 10,000 lanterns fill the night sky.\n\nThe Akita Kanto Matsuri, or Akita Kanto Festival is an annual festival held in summer in Akita City, northern Japan.\n\nTogether with the Nebuta Festival in Aomori, and the Tanabata Festival in Sendai, it is considered to be one of the Three Great Tohoku Festivals, and it certainly is one magnificent festival the Tohoku region is proud of.\n\nIt is believed that the origins of the Akita Kanto Festival could be tracked down to the old custom of neburi nagashi, often performed during the Tanabata, Obon, and other festivals of the old Japanese calendar (*1).', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/03/21-21813.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/03/21-21814.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/03/21-21810.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/03/21-21809.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2017-07-24'::timestamptz, E'Akita'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/25099', E'en', 5, E'[Akita] With over 100 years of history! Japan\'s No. 1 fireworks competition - "Omagari Fireworks Festival"', E'The Omagari Fireworks Festival is one of Japan\'s leading fireworks festivals, held annually in Daisen City (formerly Omagari City), Akita Prefecture. This festival has a long history of over 100 years. It is known as "Japan\'s No. 1 fireworks competition" due to its technical skill and high level of prestige.\n\nThe origins of this event date back to 1910 (Meiji 43), and it is a historic and prestigious event that has been handed down for over 100 years since then. Today, it is highly regarded as Japan\'s most prestigious fireworks competition, where top fireworks makers selected from around the country gather to compete in skill and artistry.\n\nThe biggest feature of Omagari Fireworks is that the fireworks are not just for viewing, but are positioned as a "competition." Only a select few fireworks companies can participate, and all the fireworks that are launched boast an extremely high level of perfection in terms of composition, color, timing, and creativity.\n\nThe event is divided into "Daytime Fireworks" and "Night Fireworks." The daytime fireworks feature a visual and technical display using the colors and shapes of the smoke.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/06/06-235675.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/06/06-235676.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/06/06-235678.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/06/06-235680.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2025-06-06'::timestamptz, E'Akita'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/25347', E'en', 6, E'Yonezawa in Yamagata: A 1-Night, 2-Day Itinerary Packed with Gourmet Eats, Nature & Onsen', E'In this article, we’ll introduce the best spots you can visit on a 1-night, 2-day trip to Yonezawa, while sharing the hidden charms of this city in Yamagata Prefecture.\n\nYonezawa City in Yamagata Prefecture is famous as a castle town with beautiful scenery throughout the four seasons. Although it is a historic town with ties to the Sengoku period warlords Uesugi Kenshin and Uesugi Yozan, there is also a wealth of attractions in various places, such as the delicious gourmet food of Yonezawa beef, Onogawa Onsen, where fireflies flutter about in season, and Tengendai Takaharu , where you can enjoy nature to the fullest. Yonezawa City, Yamagata Prefecture , can be reached from Tokyo in about two hours on the Yamagata Shinkansen. This time, we will introduce recommended spots to visit in aa 1-night, 2-day to make the most of your time in Yonezawa City . I hope that through this article you will be able to get a sense of the charms of Yonezawa City, Yamagata Prefecture that you may not yet know about. In this article, we will be focusing on gourmet food, stunning views, and hot springs.\n\nThere are rental cars, tourist buses, and taxis available near the station, so you can still enjoy sightseeing even if you can\'t drive. It would also be nice to rent a bicycle and go sightseeing, especially in spring and autumn.\n\nWhen sightseeing in Yonezawa City, we recommend renting a bicycle depending on the weather. There are three locations in the city where you can rent a bicycle: Yonezawa Station, Uesugi Count\'s Residence (Japanese Garden), and Yonezawa Roadside Station. You can also return the car anywhere between Yonezawa Station and the Uesugi Count\'s Residence (Japanese Garden), so it\'s great that you can use the service to suit your travel style. In addition, the rental bicycles are electric, making it easy to get around. Although the number is limited, some come with child seats, so you can consider renting one with small children.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/07/07-238260.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/07/07-238282.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/07/07-238278.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/07/07-238279.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2025-07-08'::timestamptz, E'Yonezawa'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/17723', E'en', 6, E'Zao Onsen Guide: Ski Resorts, Snow Monsters, and Hot Springs', E'The Zao region, nestled between Miyagi and Yamagata prefectures, offers natural marvels like the snow monsters, ancient hot springs, a fox village, and the volcanic crater lake Okama. This nature-rich paradise is ideal for outdoor enthusiasts with hiking and skiing opportunities.\n\nZao is located on the border of Yamagata and Miyagi prefectures in the Tohoku region. In recent years, it has gained popularity due to the snow monsters (juhyo) and Zao Fox Village being prominent tourist spots.\n\nIn addition to these two attractions, the Zao area in Yamagata, known for its hot springs, has nature-rich surroundings worth exploring. You can also climb an active volcano and see the volcanic crater lake known as Okama. In winter, there are excellent ski resorts to enjoy as well.\n\nHow to Get to Zao The main attractions in Zao are centered around Zao Onsen. To reach Zao Onsen, you must take a bus to Zao Onsen Bus Terminal. The bus ride from JR Yamagata Station takes about 40 minutes.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/08/18-241571.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2020/12/23-111130.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2018/08/24-61276.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/12/27-159998.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2024-01-18'::timestamptz, E'Zao Onsen'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/8000', E'en', 6, E'Yamagata Top 15 Things To Do - Ginzan Onsen, Mt. Zao, Yamadera, And More', E'Yamagata Prefecture in northern Japan is filled with exciting destinations, including retro onsen towns, frost-covered trees of Mt. Zao, and local delicacies from wagyu beef, ramen, to sake. Learn the top sightseeing spots in Yamagata and how to get there.\n\nYamagata Prefecture is located in the southern part of Japan\'s Tohoku region and faces the Sea of Japan.\n\nThe prefecture has scenic mountain and ocean views with hot springs steeped with history and culture. It\'s also where visitors can enjoy regional food and drink, including fruit, wagyu beef, ramen, and locally brewed sake.\n\nYamagata Prefecture has four main regions. The Murayama region is home to prefectural capital, Yamagata city, which boasts spring towns such as Ginzan Onsen and Zao Onsen, and delicious cherries. The Okitama region is known for its Yonezawa beef, a type of wagyu Japanese beef, among other tasty foods.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/09/30-87052.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/09/30-87056.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/09/30-87087.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/09/30-87086.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2019-10-15'::timestamptz, E'Ginzan Onsen'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/24299', E'en', 7, E'Tsuruga Castle Park Cherry Blossom Festival: Highlights and Tips', E'Tsuruga Castle Park hosts cherry blossom light-ups alongside the Tsuruga Castle Cherry Blossom Festival. Read on to learn the dates and festival highlights.\n\nTsuruga Castle (Wakamatsu Castle), located in Aizuwakamatsu City, is surrounded by approximately 1,000 cherry blossom trees, creating a magnificent scene in spring.\n\nTsuruga Castle Park has been selected among the "100 Famous Cherry Blossom Spots in Japan" for its beauty, making it a signature cherry blossom spot in Aizu.\n\nEvery year, many domestic and international tourists visit to enjoy hanami (flower viewing) during the cherry blossom season.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/02/27-226232.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/02/27-226234.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/02/27-226233.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/12/26-254092.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2025-03-11'::timestamptz, E'Aizuwakamatsu'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/23836', E'en', 7, E'Fukushima: 10 Scenic Cherry Blossom Spots to Visit in 2026', E'Fukushima Prefecture is dotted with many famous cherry blossom spots. We introduce 10 famous cherry blossom locations in Fukushima―from classic spots all the way to hidden gems.\n\nFukushima Prefecture, blessed with an abundance of natural surroundings, is dotted with some of Japan\'s best cherry blossom viewing spots.\n\nHowever, the media tends to focus exclusively on famous places such as the Meguro River (Tokyo) and Hirosaki Park (Aomori Prefecture). Visitors from outside Fukushima might not be familiar with the famous cherry blossom spots the region has to offer.\n\nSo this article introduces Fukushima\'s top 10 cherry blossom viewing locations, from classic spots all the way to little-known hideaways.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/01/31-221833.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/01/31-221835.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/01/31-221839.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/01/31-221844.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2025-02-07'::timestamptz, E'Fukushima'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/7443', E'en', 7, E'10 Things to Do in Fukushima: Sightseeing, Food, and Safety', E'Fukushima is filled with wonderful spots for travel, like Aizu Wakamatsu, Ouchijuku, Lake Inawashiro, and the Tadami Line. Read to learn about where to visit, activities, and local food. This article also introduces tips, from transportation to the latest safety information.\n\nFukushima is a prefecture located in the northern Tohoku region of Japan. With its great outdoors, delicious cuisine, and seasonal views, it is an ideal spot for you to visit again and again.\n\nSince the incidents in 2011, Fukushima is now completely safe for visitors to travel in nearly all parts of Fukushima Prefecture.\n\nIn this article, we introduce the latest information on Fukushima, along with must-visit spots, cultural events, and delicious food.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/22-77516.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/21-77424.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/20-77305.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/21-77432.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2019-06-10'::timestamptz, E'Fukushima'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/25326', E'en', 8, E'[Kanto area] Let\'s go see the autumn kochia! 4 popular spots we definitely recommend🍁', E'Autumn is the season when the beauty of nature is particularly striking. The temperature becomes mild and the air is clear and comfortable, making it the perfect time for outings and walks. The autumn leaves color the mountains, and the unique autumn scenery soothes our souls.\n\nAutumn is known as a season that offers many different ways to enjoy it. The temperature becomes milder and the air becomes clearer, making it the perfect time for outings and walks. The mountains change color and the beauty of nature is considered to be the most striking season of the year.\n\nOne plant you can\'t miss in the fall is "kochia," an annual plant known in Japanese as "hokigusa."\n\nKochia is an annual plant of the Bassia genus in the Amaranthaceae family, and its Japanese name is "hokigusa." The name comes from the fact that mature stems were dried and used as brooms in the past. Kochia is characterized by its unique round and fluffy shape, making it a very cute looking plant.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/07/03-238066.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/07/03-238089.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/07/03-238068.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/07/03-238069.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2025-07-03'::timestamptz, null),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/22139', E'en', 8, E'Mount Tsukuba Autumn Foliage: Best Spots and Fall Festivals', E'Discover Mount Tsukuba’s autumn foliage in November. Enjoy scenic hikes, cable car rides, and the Tsukuba Momiji Festival during peak fall season.\n\nMount Tsukuba, located in Ibaraki is one of the best places to enjoy hiking amid autumn foliage near Tokyo.\n\nTsukuba Station can be reached in about 40 minutes by train from Akihabara Station on the Tsukuba Express Line. From the station, you can go by local bus to the Mount Tsukuba entrance in about 20 minutes.\n\nThe ideal time to see the autumn foliage on Mount Tsukuba is typically from early to late November, with stunning scenery spreading from the summit down to the foothills.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/10/24-204865.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/10/24-204866.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/10/28-205698.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/10/24-204867.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2024-10-24'::timestamptz, E'Tsukuba'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/11536', E'en', 8, E'Ibaraki: 27 Things to Do, Culinary Delights, and Travel Tips', E'Discover 27 best things to do in Ibaraki, from Hitachi Seaside Park to hidden gems. Explore top scenic spots, local food, and travel tips near Tokyo.\n\nIbaraki is located north-east of Tokyo, bordering Chiba, Saitama, Tochigi, and Fukushima prefectures. It is the 11th largest prefecture in Japan, with a population of around 2.84 million. Mito City is the prefectural capital.\n\nHitachi Seaside Park is one of the most famous spots in the prefecture.\n\nIt is located in Hitachinaka City by the Pacific Ocean. From JR Katsuta Station, ride the bus departing from the east gate to bus stop No. 2. It is a 15-minute ride to Kaihin Koen West Entrance (Nishi-guchi) bus stop and 20 minutes to Kaihin Koen South Entrance (Minami-guchi) bus stop.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/23-77620.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/01/10-69160.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/01/18-133844.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/10/26-39710.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2023-05-09'::timestamptz, E'Ibaraki'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/22758', E'en', 9, E'Utsunomiya Food Guide: Top 10 Spots for Gyoza, Yakisoba, and Desserts', E'Utsunomiya, the largest city in the northern Kanto region, is also a foodie paradise with delicious local dishes such as Utsunomiya gyoza, yakisoba noodles, and beef. We introduce 10 restaurants and cafes where you can taste the best cuisine in Utsunomiya.\n\nUtsunomiya, a city in Tochigi Prefecture easily accessible from Tokyo, is known for its vibrant food culture.\n\nThe most famous dish is Utsunomiya gyoza, potstickers with a tasty filling of vegetables and minced meat. This signature dish originated when the 14th Division of the Japanese Imperial Army, stationed in Manchuria during the 1930s, spread gyoza they had eaten locally upon returning to their headquarters in Utsunomiya.\n\nAdditionally, you must try kabuto-age, which is a deep-fried half chicken, the B-grade gourmet classic Utsunomiya yakisoba (fried noodles), and the brand wagyu Utsunomiya beef, known for its exquisite taste—all sure to make your mouth water!', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/11/19-210042.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/11/19-210043.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/11/19-210044.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/11/19-210045.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2024-12-02'::timestamptz, E'Utsunomiya'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/7516', E'en', 9, E'Tochigi Travel Guide - 10 Things To Do In Nikko, Utsunomiya, And Nasu', E'Tochigi offers various attractions that draw visitors. Starting with Nikko, a UNESCO World Heritage site famous for its stunning shrines and temples, including Toshogu Shrine. The natural beauty of Nikko National Park with its waterfalls and scenic landscapes is also a major draw.\n\nTochigi Prefecture is located to the north of Tokyo. Accessible via a one-to-two-hour train ride from Tokyo, it is ideal for a day trip. Tochigi has plenty to offer ranging from its rich history of Nikko to the abundant nature of Kinugawa and Nasu. This article introduces both places and activities in Tochigi, as well as how to access them.\n\nThe area also has theme parks. EDO WONDERLAND, a reenactment of Edo-Priod Japan, and Tobu World Square, which reproduces famous sites around the world on a 1:25 scale, are fun for kids and adults alike.\n\nIeyasu Tokugawa, the founder of the Edo Shogunate, is enshrined in the main shrine.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/06-78884.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/05-78785.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/06-78880.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/05-78780.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2019-06-25'::timestamptz, E'Nikko'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/5412', E'en', 9, E'Edo Wonderland Nikko Edomura: 10 Activities to Enjoy in Day', E'Edo Wonderland Nikko Edomura is a cultural theme park near Nikko that recreates the atmosphere of Edo (pre-modern Tokyo). Become an Edo citizen for a day and explore the roots of Japanese culture through the amazing activities available in Edo Wonderland.\n\nAnyone planning a trip to Japan who did a bit of research on what to visit in Nikko must have heard of Edo Wonderland, an area that recreates the atmosphere of Edo (pre-modern Tokyo).\n\nAlso known as Nikko Edomura, Edo Wonderland is a samurai and ninja cultural theme park with museums, theaters, dining places, and many other facilities where visitors can have a firsthand experience of how people in Japan used to live during the Edo period (1603 - 1868).\n\nIn Edo Wonderland, you are encouraged to become an Edo citizen for a day! There is no better way to learn about the roots of Japanese culture while having fun! This article will introduce some of the activities that would make your day in Edo unforgettable.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/11/26-42260.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/11/26-42259.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/11/28-42367.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/11/26-42261.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2018-03-19'::timestamptz, E'Nikko'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/7337', E'en', 10, E'Kusatsu Onsen Guide: Best Hot Springs, Travel Tips and Where to Stay', E'Kusatsu Onsen, one of the top three hot springs in Japan, can be easily accessed by direct bus from Tokyo. Learn more about the best natural hot springs in Kusatsu, food, souvenirs, and where to stay.\n\nThere are many restaurants and cafes in Kusatsu Onsen, making the area lively even at night. Hot springs flow from the waterfall-like Yubatake in the town center, where visitors can see the large amounts of steam rising.\n\nThis article introduces the features of Kusatsu Onsen, places to visit, food, and accommodations for a relaxing hot spring trip.\n\nKusatsu Onsen is easy to access from Tokyo. You can get there in about four hours via express bus from the Shinjuku Bus Terminal.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/11/10-151777.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/old_thumbnails/200x2000/1949.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/04/24-75961.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/09-76485.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2019-05-28'::timestamptz, E'Kusatsu Onsen'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/4040', E'en', 10, E'Minakami Onsen: Best Hot Springs, Top Ryokan and Things to Do', E'Minakami Onsen is a hot spring resort in Gunma Prefecture, which can be easily reached from Tokyo in about one hour by train. An ideal destination for a day trip, it has amazing hot springs and Japanese-style inns, which we introduce here.\n\nIn recent years, there has been a growing number of activities that take advantage of the natural spots found in this region. Tone River, a river that runs along the side of Minakami where the hot springs are clustered, is a river where you can take part in rafting and canoeing. Along with the nine ski resorts here where you can ski and snowboard in winter, this is a town that has the perfect environment for everybody from kids to adults to be able to enjoy the outdoors.\n\nThe quality of water in the hot springs is different in every bath. The most notable quality of water at Minakami Onsen is the calcium sulfate spring. A calcium spring is said to be good for treating bruises and cuts, wounds such as burns, and even breakouts of acne and rashes on the skin. These waters are clear and colorless.\n\nFor those coming from Tokyo, it’s best to take the JR Joetsu shinkansen. From Tokyo Station, get on the Joetsu Toki shinkansen headed for Niigata and get off at Jomokogen Station. It takes about an hour and 10 minutes, and costs 5390 yen.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/02/15-18743.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/02/15-18744.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/02/15-18746.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/02/15-18747.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2017-11-28'::timestamptz, E'Minakami'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/11938', E'en', 10, E'Outdoor Fun and Hot Springs near Tokyo! Gunma: Top 30 Things to Do', E'Gunma, easily accessible from Tokyo, boasts famous hot spring resorts, nature, and historical sites. Read on to learn the best things to do and places to visit in Gunma, from Kusatsu Onsen to the outdoor heaven of Minakami, alongside food, hotels, and access from Tokyo.\n\nRafting in Minakami. Photo by Pixta Gunma Prefecture is a popular destination near Tokyo for those who love hot springs and spending time in the great outdoors. Gunma\'s nature-rich locations are ideal for hiking, whitewater rafting, canoeing, skiing in winter, and other outdoor activities.\n\nRead on to learn about the best things to enjoy in Gunma, from hot springs and outdoor fun to food, accessibility, hotels, and more!\n\nMost people probably think of hot springs when it comes to Gunma, don’t they? First, we’ll introduce the top four famous hot spring resorts!', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/06/05-138956.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/06/06-138998.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/06/06-138999.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/06/06-139006.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2023-05-27'::timestamptz, E'Kusatsu Onsen'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/7527', E'en', 11, E'Kawagoe: Places to Visit and Food Spots for Cultural Travel', E'Discover Kawagoe, a stunning historic district near Tokyo. From the iconic Toki no Kane bell tower to the romantic Hikawa Shrine, here are 15 must-see attractions and local street food spots.\n\nJust 30 minutes from Tokyo, Kawagoe offers a captivating glimpse into Japan’s past. Known as "Little Edo," this Saitama Prefecture gem is famous for its remarkably preserved architecture from the Edo period (1603–1868).\n\nVisitors can stroll through the historic Kurazukuri Street, lined with traditional clay-walled warehouses, and hear the iconic Toki no Kane bell tower chime throughout the day.\n\nFrom vibrant festivals to artisanal crafts and local street food, Kawagoe is the ultimate destination for those seeking a deep dive into Japanese heritage.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/10-79061.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/10-79065.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/10-79066.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/10-79067.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2023-10-26'::timestamptz, E'Kawagoe'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/8676', E'en', 11, E'Saitama: Top 12 Things to Do on a Day Trip From Tokyo', E'Cities like Kawagoe, Chichibu, or Omiya in Saitama Prefecture are excellent Tokyo day trip destinations. This article features fun things to do and places to visit in Saitama, including nature-rich destinations, historical sites, amusement parks, and museums.\n\nSaitama, the prefecture bordering Tokyo to the north and west, boasts a number of little-known yet incredibly appealing tourist attractions.\n\nThis article features 12 fun things to do and places to visit in Saitama alongside local food recommendations. All these destinations are ideal for day trips from Tokyo.\n\nToki no Kane, or the Time Bell Tower, is a belfry built over 300 years ago that has become the iconic structure of Kawagoe. The historical district is full of charming little shops selling Japanese souvenirs and tasty local treats.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/05/26-138581.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/05/26-138582.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/05/26-138577.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/03/21-21799.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2023-05-16'::timestamptz, E'Chichibu'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/26901', E'en', 11, E'Where to See Moss Phlox: 5 Top Shibazakura Spots in Japan', E'Experience the vibrant pink carpets of Japan’s moss phlox season. Discover five shibazakura locations, from a Mt. Fuji resort to hidden gems near Tokyo.\n\nOf all Japan\'s spring blooms, shibazakura (moss phlox) is a favorite for the vibrant, carpet-like landscapes it creates.\n\nLiterally translating to "lawn cherry blossoms," moss phlox flowers begin to bloom just as the traditional cherry blossom season fades, painting hillsides in shades of pink from April to May.\n\nFrom the iconic Fuji Shibazakura Festival near Mt. Fuji to hidden gems closer to Tokyo, here are five locations perfect for capturing that flawless shot.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/03/06-260524.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/03/06-260525.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/03/06-260526.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/03/13-260984.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2026-03-04'::timestamptz, null),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/7713', E'en', 12, E'Vegetarian And Vegan At Tokyo Disney! Disneyland And DisneySea Guide', E'Tokyo Disney Resort, with its imaginative rides and spectacular shows, is a must-see for many in Japan. Food is another part of the fun not to miss out on. This article lists vegetarian and vegan food at Tokyo Disneyland and Tokyo DisneySea, from Mickey-shaped frozen treats to meals.\n\nTokyo Disney Resort, comprising of the Tokyo Disneyland and DisneySea parks, is known for its fantastical rides and shows. However, food is another characteristic that makes the area so magical. From colorful drinks to Mickey Mouse-shaped desserts and meals, eating at the park is an experience in itself.\n\nWith some preparation, vegetarians and even vegans can enjoy the Disney parks and its tasty treats, too. This is a guide for vegetarian and vegan guests,* or those looking to just looking to eat less meat. Continue reading to learn more about the snack and meal choices to make the most of Disney with fun and food.\n\n*In this article, vegetarian and vegan are defined in the following way: Vegetarian: not containing meat, fish, seafood ingredients, or those derived from it. The food may include dairy products, egg, and honey. Vegan: does not contain meat, fish, seafood ingredients, or those derived from it. The food does not contain dairy products, eggs, honey, or any form of animal or fish and seafood-derived ingredient.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/07/25-82318.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/07/25-82302.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/07/24-82193.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/07/24-82194.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2019-07-24'::timestamptz, null),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/27369', E'en', 12, E'Narita Gion Festival: 5 Must-See Highlights and Experience Tour', E'Discover the Narita Gion Festival near Tokyo! Explore 5 must-see highlights and an immersive experience tour that lets you join the floats firsthand.\n\nHeld every year over a weekend in early July, the Narita Gion Festival boasts more than 300 years of history. As one of Chiba’s most iconic summer events, it draws roughly 450,000 visitors annually, transforming the entire temple town with electrifying energy and passion.\n\nSpanning three days, the festival comes alive with traditional music, lively dancing, and ornate floats, all driven by the intense energy of its participants.\n\nDeeply rooted in historic Japanese culture, the celebration unfolds in downtown Narita—just minutes from Narita Airport—making it an unmissable stop for international travelers.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/07/21-267470.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/07/21-267578.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/07/21-267474.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/07/21-267466.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2026-07-27'::timestamptz, E'Narita'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/24409', E'en', 12, E'Near Tokyo! Sakura Tulip Festa 2025: Highlights and Photogenic Spots', E'The Sakura Tulip Festival 2025 will be held in April in Sakura City, near Tokyo. This event will feature highlights such as the Windmill Festival, sightseeing boats on Lake Inbanuma, and gourmet food. We\'ll also explain details on traffic restrictions for the event day.\n\nThe venue is held at Sakura Hometown Square (Sakura Furusato Plaza). It\'s famous for its tulips in April, sunflowers in July, and cosmos in October. Additionally, the windmill located within the park is a popular spot for Instagram photos.\n\nIn this article, we will introduce the highlights, event information, and access details for the Sakura Tulip Festival 2025.\n\nThe windmill at Sakura Hometown Square was built in 1994 to celebrate the 40th anniversary of Sakura City.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/03/11-227562.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/03/11-227563.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/03/11-227564.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/03/12-227750.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2025-03-24'::timestamptz, E'Sakura'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/204', E'en', 13, E'Kagurazaka Guide: Cafes, Shrines, Charming Shops, and Events', E'Kagurazaka is a Tokyo neighborhood brimming with cafes, restaurants, and small shops. Exploring the narrow, stone-paved alleyways and roads here reveals more of this unique area with Edo-period atmosphere. Read to learn the best places to visit in Kagurazaka.\n\nDue to the blend of traditional charm and new, stylish cafes and restaurants that attract visitors from every corner of the city in the evenings, Kagurazaka is often considered Tokyo\'s "little Paris."\n\nKagurazaka has also been gathering attention as Tokyo\'s "cat town" due to its vibrant Bakeneko Festival, a cat-themed Halloween event, held here every October because Natsume Soseki (1867-1916), the author of the novel "I Am a Cat" used to live nearby.\n\nRead on to learn the best places to visit in Kagurazaka, including historical sites, popular dining spots, and events.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/07/11-140972.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2018/07/04-57704.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2018/07/04-57703.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2018/07/04-57702.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2014-06-14'::timestamptz, null),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/27200', E'en', 13, E'Tokyo\'s Adachi Fireworks Festival 2026: Date, Highlights, Tips', E'Tokyo\'s Adachi Fireworks Festival will be held on May 30! Get essential details on the 13,000-shot show near Kitasenju Station, with access and tips.\n\nFireworks are a quintessential highlight of summer in Japan, traditionally held along rivers or coastlines. Kickstarting Tokyo\'s fireworks season is the Adachi Fireworks Festival, held along the Arakawa River near Kitasenju Station.\n\nWhile historically held in July, the event was moved to late May to protect spectators from heatstroke. Read on to discover this year’s schedule, event highlights, and essential tips for avoiding post-show crowds.\n\nThe Adachi Fireworks Festival began more than a century ago, with its oldest official record dating back to the celebration of Japan\'s victory in the Russo-Japanese War in 1905.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/05/12-263957.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/05/12-263955.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/05/13-263970.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/05/13-263969.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2026-05-18'::timestamptz, E'Adachi'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/27030', E'en', 13, E'Experience Otaku Culture in Just 3 Hours! A Complete Guide to Akihabara Anime & Game Adventure Tour', E'This article introduces a popular guided tour that allows you to fully experience Akihabara\'s anime and game culture in about three hours. It efficiently takes you to maid cafes, retro games, gachapon (capsule toy) experiences, and more, making it a safe and enjoyable experience even for first-timers.\n\nTable of Contents What is the Akihabara Anime & Game Adventure Tour? Tour itinerary details Recommended for people like this For your Tokyo trip, consult TripToJapan! What is the Akihabara Anime & Game Adventure Tour? Explore the vibrant streets of Akihabara. Known as Tokyo\'s famous anime and game district, this area offers arcades, unique shops, and an authentic maid café experience. Discover hidden gems and immerse yourself in the world of unique Japanese pop culture.\n\n• A guided walking tour lasting approximately 3 hours.\n\n• Popular with foreigners as it includes an English-speaking guide.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/04/16-262788.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/04/16-262790.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/04/16-262797.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2026/04/16-262789.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2026-04-16'::timestamptz, E'Akihabara'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/1519', E'en', 14, E'The Top 8 Sightseeing Spots in Enoshima, Kanagawa', E'Enoshima is a popular travel spot next to Kamakura and a little over an hour away from Tokyo via train. With its ocean scenery, Shinto shrines, and seafood, there is much to see and do. Learn eight must-visit places in Enoshima for a memorable trip.\n\nLocated one hour and a half away from Tokyo via train and 30 minutes from Kamakura, Enoshima is a small island in Fujisawa, Kanagawa Prefecture. It is a popular tourist spot with its beautiful scenery surrounded by the ocean, historical shrines and plenty of delicious restaurants. Here are the eight spots and things to do you if you’re visiting Enoshima.\n\nEnoshima Shrine, the cornerstone of Enoshima’s history, is one of Japan\'s three major shrines enshrining Nihon-Sandai-Benzaiten (*1). It is said to have been opened in the year 552.\n\nEnoshima Shrine does not consist of one building, but of three shrines which are scattered across the island. Each of these three shrines enshrines one of the three of the Munakata-Sanjoshin (*2). Many people visit, because these are the goddesses of the sea, water, fortune and wealth. Moreover, they are said to give the blessing of increased art skills to the ones who pray to them. This is such an important place that one can probably say they’ve visited Enoshima simply by visiting Enoshima Shrine.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2022/04/18-125790.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2022/04/18-125791.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2022/04/18-125792.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2022/04/18-125793.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2016-03-17'::timestamptz, E'Enoshima'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/2591', E'en', 14, E'Things to Do in Yokohama: Top 25 Activities, Gardens, Museums & More', E'Yokohama is a bustling port city just 30 minutes by train from Tokyo. We introduce 25 exciting things to do and see in Yokohama, from scenic Minato Mirai and Cosmo World to cruises and rides with the Sea Bass, or the Yokohama Air Cabin.\n\nYokohama’s history began to take shape in 1859 when it became one of the first ports opened after Japan’s period of isolation. Before this, Yokohama was just a small fishing village. However, after the port was opened, Western culture and technology flowed in, and Yokohama rapidly developed into an important international city that played a key role in Japan’s modernization.\n\nDuring the Meiji period, Yokohama saw the construction of buildings reflecting Western influences, such as the Red Brick Warehouse and the foreign settlement areas, which contributed to the flourishing trade centered around Yokohama Port. The first railway line, linking Shimbashi and Yokohama, was also established during this period, marking a key step in the development of Japan’s transportation network.\n\nOne of Yokohama\'s prominent attractions is its Chinatown, the largest in Japan, where visitors can indulge in delicious Chinese cuisine and explore the colorful streets lined with traditional Chinese architecture. The Yokohama Red Brick Warehouse, the former Customs Inspection House has been transformed into a shopping complex, and is another popular attraction offering a variety of dining options and specialty stores.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/06/30-140312.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/06/30-140326.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/06/28-140051.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2023/06/30-140336.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2016-10-01'::timestamptz, E'Yokohama'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/25049', E'en', 14, E'THE OUTLETS SHONAN HIRATSUKA Guide: Shopping, Outlet Appliances, and Local Flavors', E'THE OUTLETS SHONAN HIRATSUKA is an outlet mall where you can experience the local charm of Shonan, with electronics stores, a park, a gorgeous view of Mt. Fuji, and local gourmet. Read on for an in-depth look at what it has to offer.\n\nPlanning to trip outside of Tokyo? It’s always nice to find a place where you can enjoy both shopping and the unique charm of the area. THE OUTLETS SHONAN HIRATSUKA, a new shopping complex developed by the AEON Group, offers just that.\n\nTHE OUTLETS SHONAN HIRATSUKA offers a wide variety of brands, with a stunning view of Mt. Fuji, local gourmet food, and a grassy plaza where children can play freely.\n\nNow, we’ll take a closer look at what THE OUTLETS SHONAN HIRATSUKA has to offer.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/06/02-235061.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/06/02-235062.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/06/02-235063.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/06/02-235064.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2025-06-28'::timestamptz, null),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/7341', E'en', 15, E'Niigata Travel Guide: 15 Must-Visit Places, Food, And Amazing Photo Spots', E'Niigata extends north to south facing the Sea of Japan. Visitors love it for its delicious rice, sake, and seafood, along with its ski resorts and many breathtaking views. This guide introduces Niigata foods, fun activities and photogenic places within the prefecture.\n\nNiigata is a treasure trove of amazing sights and regional cuisine. Renowned as Japan’s leading producer of top-notch rice and Japanese sake, you can also expect to eat amazing seafood here because Niigata Prefecture overlooks the Sea of Japan.\n\nNiigata is located in the Chubu region of Japan\'s main island Honshu. Stretching long and thin from north to south, the prefecture is largely split into three areas starting from the west called Joetsu, Chuetsu, and Kaetsu, respectively.\n\nThere are direct flights operating from Taiwan Taoyuan International Airport to Niigata Airport, making it easily accessible for overseas visitors. For those traveling by train, it takes about two hours via the shinkansen (bullet train) from JR Tokyo Station to Niigata Station, which also makes it convenient to visit while traveling through Japan.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/08-76424.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/08-76401.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/08-76399.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/05/08-76400.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2019-05-26'::timestamptz, E'Niigata'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/25331', E'en', 15, E'[Kanto Autumn Foliage] Autumn travel season has arrived! Stunning autumn spots to visit around the Kanto region', E'With the arrival of autumn, the mountains and parks around the Kanto region are colored with beautiful autumn leaves. October is especially good for viewing the red and yellow leaves, and many people visit to enjoy the vibrant natural scenery.\n\nIn Japan, mountains and roadside trees turn red and yellow in autumn, and the custom of "momijigari" has been popular since ancient times. Along with cherry blossom viewing in spring, enjoying autumn leaves is an important part of culture to feel the change of seasons. In particular, from October to November, when maple and ginkgo trees take on beautiful colors, many people visit famous autumn foliage spots and are soothed by the beauty of nature. It is also a popular destination for travel and day trips, and temples, shrines, parks, and mountain areas are often illuminated and have events.\n\nAutumn leaves are not just a beautiful sight, but an important seasonal feature that allows Japanese people to "savor autumn."\n\nBy the way, do you know the difference between "momiji" and "momiji (maple)"? These two words are often used interchangeably, but in fact they each have a different meaning.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/07/07-238244.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/07/03-238115.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/07/03-238116.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/07/03-238117.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2025-07-07'::timestamptz, null),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/22718', E'en', 15, E'Niigata Ski Guide: 11 Ski Resorts Where Even Beginners Can Have Fun!', E'In this article, we feature 11 ski resorts in the Yuzawa area of Niigata with good access from Tokyo. Families with kids and novice skiers can all have fun at unique resorts that also offer night skiing. Please refer to this article as you anticipate the upcoming ski season!\n\nNiigata Prefecture\'s Yuzawa Town is an area with great access from the Kanto Region. The Kan\'etsu Expressway\'s Yuzawa Interchange (IC) and the Joetsu shinkansen\'s Echigo Yuzawa Station are both nearby.\n\nThe region is also well known for its excellent snow quality which attracts many skiers. Many of the area\'s ski hills also offer lift tickets at reasonable prices.\n\nThis article features eleven noteworthy ski resorts from the many found in this area.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/11/18-209946.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/11/18-209947.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/11/18-209950.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/11/18-209966.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2024-11-29'::timestamptz, E'Echigo Yuzawa'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/9722', E'en', 16, E'Tateyama Kurobe Alpine Route Guide - Bask In Japan\'s Great Outdoors', E'The Tateyama Kurobe Alpine Route is a mountainous destination spanning 3,000 meters that links Toyama and Nagano prefectures. Read about its spectacular sights, including the caldera lake Mikurigaike and the powerful Kurobe Dam. Learn how to get to this famous nature area and things to do.\n\nSeventy percent of Japan\'s terrain is made up of mountains and hills. Thus, outdoor destinations are in abundance, and must-visit places are paradises for nature lovers.\n\nThe route has two starting points: Tateyama Station in Toyama and Ogizawa Station in Nagano. Visitors can go sightseeing from either point via the cable car, ropeway, or bus. Every spot along the journey is scattered with amazing sights to see. Some of them include Mikurigaike Pond, which beautifully reflects the mountains in its waters, and Kurobe Dam, the tallest dam in Japan! We suggest researching the places beforehand and double-checking the modes of transportation available.\n\nIn this article, we\'ll introduce these scenic spots along the Tateyama Kurobe Alpine Route and how to get to them.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2020/04/27-102153.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2020/04/27-102151.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2020/04/27-102132.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2020/04/27-102130.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2020-05-16'::timestamptz, E'Tateyama Kurobe Alpine Route'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/5368', E'en', 16, E'Cycling In Toyama - Bayside And Rural Sightseeing Spots', E'Toyama is famous for its natural settings and cultural assets. This article introduces two cycling courses which allow the participants to enjoy making sushi or visiting a unique temple.\n\nIn Toyama Prefecture, visitors can enjoy both the great view of Toyama Bay and the idyllic view of the mountains and the rural areas. To fully enjoy its appeals, cycling is the recommended way to move around in Toyama.\n\nIn this article, we will introduce the various sightseeing spots along the Toyama Bay Cycling Course and Den-en Cycling Course.\n\nPhotograph provided by Toyama Prefecture', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/11/15-41440.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/11/15-41441.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/11/15-41442.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2017/11/15-41443.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2017-12-28'::timestamptz, E'Toyama'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/2728', E'en', 16, E'Visit Doraemon\'s Hometown! Takaoka City in Toyama Prefecture', E'Doraemon is one of the most popular anime characters in Japan. If you like him or have grown up watching Doraemon\'s adventures, visit Takaoka city in Toyama prefecture, the place where Doraemon\'s creator, Fujiko F.Fujio, was born. We feature three Takaoka locations related to Doraemon.\n\nWith the opening of the Hokuriku bullet train line in 2015, the Hokuriku region became a popular destination for many travelers.\n\nDid you know that the city of Takaoka in Toyama prefecture is not only a great travel destination but also the hometown of Doraemon\'s creator, Fujiko F. Fujio?\n\nMany places that appear in the popular manga and anime series have been recreated in Takaoka. The town is overflowing with Doraemon-related things and spots. It\'s a must-go place for Doraemon fans!', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/01/30-221643.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2025/01/30-221644.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/archive_files/jp/2016/09/e7a200f7d9e3e6690544bc9d1327eee9.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/archive_files/jp/2016/09/b6ce0054c49f2f10562589227854beb6.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2016-10-19'::timestamptz, E'Takaoka'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/7545', E'en', 17, E'Ishikawa Travel Guide: 14 Places to Visit, Fine Food, and Activities', E'Ishikawa Prefecture boasts traditional townscapes and a rich natural environment. Read on to learn about the fine cuisine, scenic places to visit, and cultural assets of Kanazawa, Noto, and Kaga Onsen in Ishikawa.\n\nIshikawa Prefecture is located in the Hokuriku Region. Following the debut of the Hokuriku Shinkansen, day trips from Tokyo to this prefecture have become popular.\n\nIshikawa is famous for its natural environment and culture, with a wide variety of sightseeing spots. It is also renowned for its fresh seafood, such as the crabs caught in the Japan Sea, and adorable Japanese confectionery.\n\nTraditional craftwork, namely lacquerware and gold leaf, is produced in Wajima City. For those hoping to experience fine cuisine and Japanese culture, Ishikawa is the place to visit.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/12-79333.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/12-79309.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/12-79310.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2019/06/12-79332.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2019-07-01'::timestamptz, E'Kanazawa'),
(E'https://matcha-jp.com/en/20689', E'en', 17, E'Kaga: 15 Things to Do For a Culture-Filled Experience', E'Kaga, located in Ishikawa prefecture, is known for its relaxing onsen towns, traditional crafts, and historical sites. In this article, we will introduce 12 exciting things to do to get the most out of your visit!\n\nKaga is a city located in Ishikawa Prefecture known for its serene hot spring towns, traditional crafts like pottery, and historical sites like Daishoji (an Edo period castle town).\n\nKaga holds a rich culture and history that attracts all types of visitors. Whether you’re a group of five or traveling solo, you will find an activity suited for you or your group.\n\nIf you’re searching for what to do and where to go, look no further, we’ve got you covered! Choose from one of these activities in Kaga and create memories you’ll be talking about years after.', E'["https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/07/23-189299.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/07/23-189317.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/07/23-189318.webp","https://resources.matcha-jp.com/resize/720x2000/2024/07/23-189319.webp"]'::jsonb, E'2024-07-23'::timestamptz, E'Kaga')
